For the basic graph $y=x^{n}$, an **even** power gives a **turning point** at the origin, with both arms rising and line symmetry about the $y$-axis.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-3.2,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.33:1.33,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[right,font=\small] at (0.3,-0.8) {turning point};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For the basic graph $y=x^{n}$, an **odd** power gives a point of inflection at the origin, where the concavity changes, with the arms going opposite ways and point symmetry about the origin.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-3.2,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.25:1.25,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[right,font=\small] at (0.35,-0.9) {point of inflection};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

